## INTRODUCTION

Biological databases and bioinformatics tools are essential resources for storing, retrieving, managing, and analyzing biological data. 
They provide access to information on genes, proteins, genomes, molecular structures, metabolic pathways, and evolutionary relationships.
These databases and tools help researchers perform sequence analysis, functional annotation, multiple sequence alignment, phylogenetic
analysis, structural visualization, and comparative genomics. They are widely used in genomics, proteomics, molecular biology,
biotechnology, medicine, agriculture, and drug discovery, making biological research more efficient, accurate, and data-driven.

